/* data/learn-c/phy-11-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-05"] = {"5.1":{"s":"Gravitation is the attraction between any two masses. It explains why objects fall, why the Moon goes round the Earth and the planets go round the Sun. This chapter covers Kepler's laws of planetary motion, Newton's law of gravitation, how g changes with height, depth and latitude, gravitational potential energy, escape speed and the motion of satellites.","w":["The force of gravity between two masses is mutual and equal in size. The Earth pulls the apple as much as the apple pulls the Earth.","G (universal constant) is not g (acceleration due to gravity)."],"x":["Gravitation is the attraction that every mass has for every other mass. It makes an apple fall, it keeps the Moon in its orbit round the Earth, and it keeps the planets in orbit round the Sun. It is the weakest of the four basic forces, but it acts over very large distances, and it is always attractive, so it controls the motion of large bodies.","This chapter follows the story in order. First, Kepler's three laws, which describe how the planets move. Then, Newton's law of gravitation, which explains why they move in this way. Then the acceleration due to gravity g, and how it changes with height, depth and latitude. Then gravitational potential energy, the escape speed, and the motion of satellites.","Study tip: most numericals use only a few results: F = Gm₁m₂/r², g = GM/R², v_escape = √(2gR), v_orbit = √(GM/r), and T² ∝ r³. Learn when to use each."],"e":"The Moon goes round the Earth because the gravitational pull of the Earth gives it the centripetal acceleration needed for a circle. If the pull stopped, the Moon would go off in a straight line."},"5.2":{"s":"Kepler's three laws describe planetary motion. First: a planet moves in an ellipse with the Sun at one focus. Second: the line from the Sun to the planet sweeps equal areas in equal times, so a planet moves faster when it is nearer the Sun; this follows from conservation of angular momentum. Third: the square of the period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis.","f":[{"x":"Delta A/Delta t = L/(2*m) = constant","n":"Law of areas: L = angular momentum of the planet of mass m; the area swept per unit time is constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.2, p.79"},{"x":"T^2 proportional to r^3","n":"Law of periods: T = time for one revolution, r = semi-major axis. So T^2/r^3 is the same for all planets.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.2, p.80"}],"w":["In T² ∝ r³ the r is the SEMI-MAJOR axis, which is the average distance of the planet from the Sun."],"x":["Kepler found three laws from the measurements of the positions of the planets. First law (law of orbits): a planet moves in an ellipse, and the Sun is at one of the foci. Second law (law of areas): the line from the Sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal times. Third law (law of periods): the square of the time period of a planet is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of its orbit, T² ∝ a³.","The second law means that a planet moves faster when it is near the Sun, and slower when it is far. It follows from the conservation of angular momentum, since the force of the Sun is along the line to the planet, and gives no torque. The areal velocity is dA/dt = L/(2m), a constant.","The third law, T² ∝ r³, lets you compare two planets. If one planet has an orbit four times as large, the time period is 4^(3/2) = 8 times as long. The semi-major axis is the average distance from the Sun, and for a circle, it is the radius.","Common mistake: using the closest distance, instead of the semi-major axis, in T² ∝ a³. Another mistake: putting the Sun at the centre of the ellipse. It is at a focus."],"e":"Mars has a year of 1.88 Earth years. Its orbit radius, from T² ∝ r³, is r = (1.88)^(2/3) = 1.52 times that of the Earth, which agrees with the measured value of 1.52 AU."},"5.3":{"s":"Newton's universal law of gravitation: the attraction between two particles grows with the product of their masses and falls as the square of their separation. The force acts along the line joining them, and the two forces form an action-reaction pair. G is the universal gravitational constant. A uniform hollow shell exerts no force on a mass inside it, and acts like a point mass at its centre for a mass outside.","f":[{"x":"F = G*m_1*m_2/r^2","n":"Gravitational force between two point masses m_1 and m_2 a distance r apart; G = 6.67 × 10^-11 N m^2 kg^-2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.3, p.82"}],"w":["Doubling the distance makes the force one fourth, not one half.","Inside a hollow uniform shell the net gravitational force is zero."],"x":["Newton's law of gravitation: every particle attracts every other particle with a force that is proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. In symbols, F = Gm₁m₂/r², where G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻² is the universal gravitational constant. The force acts along the line joining the two particles. The two forces on the two bodies are equal and opposite, an action-reaction pair, and they act whatever the medium between them.","For spheres of uniform density, we can take all of the mass to be at the centre. If the distance is doubled, the force falls to one fourth. If one of the masses is doubled, the force doubles. For a hollow uniform shell, the net force on a body inside it is zero, and for a body outside, it is the same as if all the mass of the shell were at its centre.","The law agreed with Kepler's laws. For a circular orbit, put the gravitational force equal to the centripetal force, and you get T² ∝ r³, which is Kepler's third law. This was a strong proof that the law is right.","Common mistake: using the radius, in place of the distance between the centres, for two spheres. Another mistake: thinking that the force on the lighter body is less. Both forces are the same."],"e":"Two masses, 10 kg and 20 kg, are 1.0 m apart. F = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 200/1 = 1.33 × 10⁻⁸ N. This is a very tiny force, so we do not notice the attraction between the objects around us."},"5.4":{"s":"The value of G cannot be found from Newton's law alone. It is measured with the Cavendish balance, in which two small masses hang on a light rod from a fine wire, and two large masses are brought near them. The tiny gravitational pull twists the wire a little. The twist is measured with a beam of light reflected from a small mirror on the wire, which gives G.","f":[{"x":"F = G m₁m₂ / r²","n":"Newton’s law of gravitation"}],"w":["G is a tiny number, 6.67 times ten to the power minus 11 in SI. That is why gravity between everyday objects cannot be felt.","Do not confuse G with g; g depends on the planet, G does not."],"x":["Newton's law gives the force in terms of G, but it does not give the value of G. G must be found by an experiment. It was first measured in the laboratory by Cavendish in 1798, with a torsion balance.","The apparatus: two small lead spheres are fixed at the ends of a light horizontal rod, which hangs from a fine quartz or metal wire. Two large lead spheres are brought near the small ones, on opposite sides. The gravitational attraction between each small sphere and the large one near it gives a couple, and the wire twists through a small angle. A beam of light reflected from a small mirror on the wire magnifies this small twist on a scale.","The twist is proportional to the torque, and the constant of the wire is found separately. So the force between the spheres is found, and then G is calculated from F = Gm₁m₂/r², since all the other quantities are known. The result is G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².","Once G is known, the mass of the Earth can be found from g = GM/R². This is why the experiment is also called the weighing of the Earth. Common mistake: thinking that G changes from place to place. It is a universal constant, and g is not."],"e":"From g = GM/R², the mass of the Earth is M = gR²/G = 9.8 × (6.4 × 10⁶)²/6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ = 6.0 × 10²⁴ kg."},"5.5":{"s":"The Earth attracts every object with a force, and the acceleration this causes, the acceleration due to gravity g, is the force per unit mass. For a point on the Earth's surface all the Earth's mass can be treated as if it were at the centre. So g depends on the mass and radius of the Earth, and not on the mass of the falling object, which is why heavy and light bodies fall together.","f":[{"x":"g = G*M/R^2","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at the Earth's surface: M = mass and R = radius of the Earth.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.5, p.85"}],"w":["g does not depend on the mass of the falling body."],"x":["The Earth attracts every body towards its centre. The acceleration that this force gives to a freely falling body is the acceleration due to gravity, g. It is also the gravitational force per unit mass. For a body of mass m at the surface, mg = GMm/R², so g = GM/R², where M and R are the mass and the radius of the Earth.","For a point on or outside the Earth, all the mass of the Earth can be taken to be at its centre. The value near the surface is about 9.8 m/s². Notice that m cancels out. So g does not depend on the mass of the falling body, and a heavy body and a light body fall together, if the air is not present.","The weight of a body is the force of gravity on it, W = mg. It depends on g, so it changes from place to place. The mass, the amount of matter, does not change. On the Moon, g is about 1/6 of that on the Earth, so your weight is 1/6, but your mass is the same.","To compare planets, use g ∝ M/R². Common mistake: using R as the height above the ground. R is the distance from the centre."],"e":"A planet has 1/10 of the Earth's mass and 1/2 of its radius. g ∝ M/R² gives g = (0.1/0.25) × 9.8 = 0.4 × 9.8 = 3.9 m/s². A 50 kg person would weigh about 196 N there, compared with 490 N on the Earth."},"5.6":{"s":"The value of g is not the same everywhere. It decreases with height above the surface and with depth below it, and it is least at the Earth's centre, where it is zero. It also changes with latitude: the Earth's rotation and its slightly flattened shape make g smallest at the equator and largest at the poles. For small heights and depths, the changes are close to linear.","f":[{"x":"g_h = g*(1 - 2*h/R)","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at a small height h above the surface (h much smaller than R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.86"},{"x":"g_d = g*(1 - d/R)","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at a depth d below the surface, with uniform density; zero at the centre (d = R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.87"},{"x":"g = (4/3)*pi*R*rho*G","n":"g at the surface in terms of the mean density rho of the Earth.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.87"},{"x":"g' = g - R*omega^2*cos^2(theta)","n":"Effective g at latitude theta, allowing for the Earth's rotation at angular speed omega; the reduction is greatest at the equator, zero at the poles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.89"}],"w":["g falls with height as 1/r² but with depth in a straight line: g is greatest at the surface.","At the poles the rotation has no effect on g; at the equator it reduces g by Rω²."],"x":["The value of g is not the same everywhere. With height h above the surface, g falls as the inverse square of the distance from the centre: g_h = gR²/(R + h)². For small h, g_h ≈ g(1 − 2h/R). With depth d below the surface, for a uniform Earth, g_d = g(1 − d/R). It decreases in a straight line, and is zero at the centre. The greatest value is at the surface.","Inside the Earth, only the mass in the sphere of radius r pulls on a body, and the shell outside gives no force. So g is proportional to r inside, and to 1/r² outside. At the surface, g = (4/3)πρGR for a uniform density ρ.","g also changes with latitude. The rotation of the Earth reduces the value by Rω² cos²λ at the latitude λ, so it is the least at the equator (reduced by Rω² = 0.034 m/s²), and there is no reduction at the poles. Also, the Earth is a little flattened, and the radius at the poles is smaller, so g is bigger at the poles.","Common mistake: using the straight line law for height, or the inverse square law for depth. For height, the law is 1/r². For depth, it is a straight line."],"e":"At a height of R above the surface, g_h = g/4 = 2.45 m/s². At a depth of R/4 below the surface, g_d = 0.75 g = 7.35 m/s². At a depth of R/2, it is 0.5 g."},"5.7":{"s":"Gravitational potential energy of a mass m at distance r from the Earth's centre is minus GMm/r, taking it as zero at infinity. It is negative because the force is attractive. Near the surface, the gain in energy on rising a small height h is mgh. Gravitational potential is the potential energy per unit mass, minus GM/r. The escape speed is the least speed at which an object can leave the Earth for ever, about 11.2 km/s.","f":[{"x":"U(r) = -G*M*m/r","n":"Gravitational potential energy of mass m at distance r from mass M (zero at infinity).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.90"},{"x":"Delta U = m*g*h","n":"Gain in potential energy for a small height h above the surface (h much smaller than R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.91"},{"x":"V = U/m = -G*M/r","n":"Gravitational potential at distance r from mass M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.91"},{"x":"v_e = sqrt(2*G*M/R) = sqrt(2*g*R)","n":"Escape speed from the surface of a planet of mass M and radius R; for the Earth 11.2 km/s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.92"}],"w":["Potential energy is negative: a bound system has total energy below zero.","The escape speed does not depend on the mass of the object that escapes."],"x":["A body of mass m, at the distance r from the Earth's centre, has the gravitational potential energy U = −GMm/r, with the zero taken at infinity. It is negative, because the force is attractive, and work must be done to take the body to infinity. The gravitational potential at that point is V = U/m = −GM/r. Near the surface, the gain of potential energy for a small height h is mgh.","The escape speed is the least speed for which a body can go from the surface to infinity and never return. At the surface, the kinetic energy must be enough to make the total energy zero: (1/2)mv² − GMm/R = 0. So v_e = √(2GM/R) = √(2gR). For the Earth, it is about 11.2 km/s. It does not depend on the mass of the body, or on the direction of projection, if the air is neglected.","A system that is bound has a negative total energy. If the total energy is zero or positive, the body escapes. This is why the Moon has no atmosphere, since the gas molecules can reach the low escape speed of 2.4 km/s, and why the Earth has kept it.","Common mistake: using +GMm/r for the potential energy, or giving a speed to the body that depends on its mass. The escape speed is the same for all masses."],"e":"For the Earth, v_e = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) = 11.2 km/s. The potential energy of a 2 kg mass on the surface is −GMm/R = −1.25 × 10⁸ J."},"5.8":{"s":"A satellite stays in orbit when the gravitational pull on it provides exactly the centripetal force needed for its circular motion. This fixes its critical (orbital) speed, which falls as the orbit gets higher and does not depend on the satellite's mass. The period increases with the radius of the orbit: T² is proportional to the cube of the orbit radius. Inside a satellite everything is in free fall, so astronauts feel weightless.","f":[{"x":"v_c = sqrt(G*M/(R + h))","n":"Critical (orbital) speed at height h above the surface; close to the surface v_c = sqrt(g*R) = 7.92 km/s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.8, p.93"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt((R + h)^3/(G*M))","n":"Period of a satellite in a circular orbit of radius R + h; T^2 is proportional to (R + h)^3.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.8, p.95"},{"x":"N = m*(g + a) (lift up), N = m*(g - a) (lift down)","n":"Apparent weight in a lift accelerating upward or downward with acceleration a; it is zero in free fall (a = g).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.8, p.95"}],"w":["Weightlessness in a satellite does not mean gravity is absent: the satellite and astronaut are both falling freely.","A satellite with speed below the critical speed spirals down, and above the escape speed it leaves the Earth."],"x":["A satellite moves in a circular orbit when the gravitational pull of the Earth gives exactly the centripetal force needed: GMm/r² = mv²/r, where r = R + h. This gives the critical (orbital) speed v_c = √(GM/r). It does not depend on the mass of the satellite, and it is smaller for a higher orbit. Very close to the surface, v_c = √(gR) = 7.9 km/s.","The time period is T = 2πr/v = 2π√(r³/GM), which agrees with Kepler's third law. Near the surface, T is about 84 minutes. A satellite whose period is 24 hours, going from west to east in the equatorial plane, is a geostationary satellite. It stays above the same point of the Earth, at a height of about 36 000 km. It is used for communication and for weather watch.","The total energy of a satellite in a circular orbit is E = −GMm/(2r), which is negative, as it is bound. If its speed is above v_c, but below the escape speed, its orbit is an ellipse. At the escape speed (√2 times v_c), it leaves the Earth.","Astronauts in a satellite feel weightless, because they and the satellite fall freely with the same acceleration, and there is no force between them and the floor. Gravity has not gone. Common mistake: saying that there is no gravity at the height of the satellite."],"e":"A satellite at a height equal to R has r = 2R. Its period is T = 2π√(8R/g) = 3.99 hours, which is 2^(3/2) times the period near the surface. Its speed is v_c/√2 = 5.6 km/s."}};
